---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the difference between pixels and KB?', a: 'Pixels are the image’s dimensions (for example 413 × 531). KB is the file size on disk. A form can limit either or both, and you change them with different tools: Resize Image for pixels, Compress Image for KB.' },
  { q: 'Does DPI matter for online uploads?', a: 'Usually not on its own. Upload checks typically look at pixel dimensions and file size. When a portal gives sizes in centimetres or inches, convert them to pixels using the DPI it states.' },
  { q: 'Why does the portal reject my photo even though it’s the right size?', a: 'Common reasons: wrong file type (PNG instead of JPG), size outside a minimum–maximum range, wrong aspect ratio, or a background colour rule. Re-read the instructions carefully.' },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="photo-signature-size-for-online-applications" faqs={faqs}>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> read the exact rules on the official application portal, then use <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a> to set the pixel dimensions and a max KB, or <a href="/compress-image">Compress Image</a> if only the file size matters. Requirements differ between organisations and change from year to year, so <strong>always check the official portal; limits change</strong>.</p>

  <p>
    This guide doesn’t list official numbers for specific exams or employers, because they’re updated often and a wrong number costs you an
    application. Instead, it explains how to read any requirement and meet it exactly.
  </p>

  <h2>The four things a requirement can specify</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Dimensions:</strong> in pixels (“200 × 230 px”), or in physical units (“3.5 × 4.5 cm”) with a resolution (“at 200 DPI”).</li>
    <li><strong>File size:</strong> a maximum (“under 50KB”) and sometimes a minimum (“at least 10KB”).</li>
    <li><strong>Format:</strong> usually JPG/JPEG. Some portals reject PNG or a file renamed from PNG to .jpg.</li>
    <li><strong>Content rules:</strong> background colour, face size, recent photo, signature in black ink on white paper, and so on.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Converting physical sizes to pixels</h2>
  <p>If the portal gives centimetres or inches, convert with this formula:</p>
  <p><strong>pixels = size in inches × DPI</strong> (and 1 inch = 2.54 cm)</p>
  <p>
    Example: a photo 3.5 cm wide at 300 DPI is 3.5 ÷ 2.54 × 300 ≈ 413 pixels. That’s why PDFNU’s “Passport 35×45 mm” preset is 413 × 531 px.
    If your portal states a different DPI, use its number instead.
  </p>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>Meeting a photo requirement, step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Take the photo against a plain background in even light, with your face centred and some space above your head.</li>
    <li>Open <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a> and select the photo.</li>
    <li>Type the required width and height. If the shape differs from your photo, choose <strong>Crop to fill</strong> and check the preview to make sure your head isn’t cut off.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>JPG</strong> and enter the maximum KB in <strong>Max size</strong>.</li>
    <li>Resize, download, and compare the file’s pixel size and KB against the rules once more.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Meeting a signature requirement</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Sign with a dark pen on plain white paper, larger than you normally would.</li>
    <li>Photograph or scan it in good light, filling the frame.</li>
    <li>In <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a>, set the required dimensions. Signatures are usually wide strips, so choose <strong>Add white border</strong> if your crop is a different shape.</li>
    <li>Set the max KB and resize. If there’s only a KB rule, <a href="/compress-image">Compress Image</a> with the 20KB or 30KB chip is quicker.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>If there’s a minimum size</h2>
  <p>
    Some portals reject files that are too small, for example “between 10KB and 50KB”. If your result is under the minimum, resize again without a
    max KB, or with a higher one. Larger pixel dimensions or higher quality both increase the file size.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep your documents private</h2>
  <p>
    Your photo and signature are identity data. PDFNU resizes and compresses them inside your browser, so they’re never uploaded to us or anyone else.
    The only place they go is the official portal you upload them to.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
